        "findings": [
          {
            "source_quote": "καὶ Ὠρίωνα τὸν ἐξ Εὐρυάλης τῆς Μίνω δι’ Εὐρυάλην τὴν μητέρα τῶν ἄστρων κατηξίωσεν",
            "english_quote": "and counted Orion, born of Euryale daughter of Minos, worthy of the stars because of Euryale his mother.",
            "issue": "The main rendering defensibly takes τῶν ἄστρων as the genitive complement of κατηξίωσεν and understands τὴν μητέρα as Orion's mother. However, the notes do not disclose the material attachment alternative: τῶν ἄστρων can instead qualify τὴν μητέρα, yielding Euryale as 'the mother of the stars,' with the celestial object of worthiness understood from context. The preceding statement that Orion was born of Euryale supports the draft's choice, but does not eliminate this alternative. This is an uncertainty-disclosure deficiency, not an unsupported main rendering or a source-verification problem.",
            "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted note explaining that the main text attaches τῶν ἄστρων to κατηξίωσεν, which can govern a genitive expressing what someone is deemed worthy of, and takes 'the mother' as Orion's mother because the preceding phrase identifies him as born of Euryale. Preserve the alternative construction: 'he deemed Orion worthy because of Euryale, the mother of the stars,' explaining that this attaches τῶν ἄστρων to μητέρα and leaves the celestial object of worthiness implicit. The amended passage and note require fresh review.",
            "severity": "major"
          }
